iOS native views: the app's UIView shown beneath GPUI's non-opaque CAMetalLayer, through gpui_apple's hole (needs 0048 and 0051).

What:
- `IosWindow::attach_native_view` puts the view in a clipping container inserted directly below GPUI's view; `draw`
  plans each scene's placements (gpui_apple's shared `Placement`, 0051, from core's `native_view_placement`,
  0048: `visible`, `background`), presents with `draw_placing_native_views` (in step whenever the scene moves,
  resizes, shows, hides or restacks a view) and then applies them in one no-animation block (frames, hidden, and
  `NativeView::set_background`'s colour on the host view behind them), then restacks, as macOS's native_views.rs
  does. A view is hidden in a scene that doesn't paint it and removed when its last handle drops.
- `native_view_input` is `Window`: GPUI's view stays on top and takes every touch, so pointer and scroll over a view
  reach its element's listeners; the view gets none unless the app forwards it.
- Rotation: GPUI's view and the containers live in a host view inside the view controller's plain root view. In
  `handle_layout_change` (inside UIKit's rotation animation) the host takes the new size without animation and only
  its centre follows the turn, and the frame at the new size is drawn right there, in the same transaction, so GPUI's
  content, the holes and the views turn as one rigid picture instead of GPUI's last frame stretching while the views
  jump. `viewWillTransitionToSize:withTransitionCoordinator:` marks the turn (`Rotation::Turning` until the
  coordinator's completion), during which the holes are cut a pixel inside the views (core's `hole_inset`), since
  Core Animation resamples the turning edges; the frame after the turn cuts them exactly again. Display Zoom
  (`nativeScale` != `scale`) gets the same inset.
- Keyboard: `set_focused_native_view` gives keyboard input to a native view (`KeyboardInput::NativeView(id)`):
  GPUI's text input view resigns and never becomes first responder, so the app can make its own UIView (a
  UITextField, a WKWebView) first responder. When GPUI focus comes back, a focused GPUI field takes first responder
  again, even though UIKit cleared its keyboard wish when the app's view took first responder from it. Dropping the
  focused view's last handle also gives the keyboard back, whatever GPUI tells the window, so a GPUI field focused
  next always gets it.

Where: everything is in new files behind one-line hooks in upstream's window.rs, text_input.rs and ios.rs:
ios/window/native_views.rs (attach, plan, apply), ios/window/rotation.rs (the nesting, the turn, the inset, the
resized frame) and ios/native_view_state.rs (the two lifecycles, `KeyboardInput` and `Rotation`, as pure
transitions, table-tested on the host by scripts/patch-test.sh, `ios-pure`).

Why the nesting is always there, not only while views show: GPUI's CAMetalLayer is its view's own backing layer, so a
native view can only go beneath it as a sibling in a superview, and upstream makes GPUI's view the view controller's
root. Creating the nesting for the first view would swap the view controller's view and move GPUI's view, with its
text input view (the first responder while a GPUI field has the keyboard), into new superviews of a live window
each time the first view comes or the last goes. Two plain views that draw nothing cost no frame; every app gets the
rigid turn.

Why: upstream's `NativeView::new` fails on iOS. Lifecycle, clipping and z-order follow Flutter's
FlutterPlatformViewsController.mm (oracle only: Flutter draws over its views, this draws under them; its touch
interceptor is the model for an app that forwards touches into its view). Flutter's FlutterViewController.mm
(`viewWillTransitionToSize:withTransitionCoordinator:`) only delays its viewport update to halfway through a turn,
which halves the stretch; rendering the new size at once keeps the views in step instead. The layout code is
gpui_macos's window/native_views.rs (upstream GPUI, Apache-2.0) adapted to UIKit, which is most of this patch's size.

Checked on the iOS 26.5 simulator (iPhone 17 at 3x, iPad Air 11-inch at 2x) with examples/native-view-apple and
scripts/apple-native-view-checks.sh: 0 GPUI frames/s while the view recolours at 63 frames/s; every recorded frame
shows the view and the div over it with no gap pixel, still, with the slot moving every frame and through a rotation
to landscape and back; after the native UITextField took the keyboard and every native view was dropped, typing goes
to GPUI's field.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

